    states: ["WY"],
    bodyText:
      "[Optional.] Conduct covered. Tenant, every other occupant of the Premises, and every guest or other person who is on the Premises or the property with Tenant's or an occupant's consent, will not engage in, facilitate or permit criminal activity on the Premises, in the building, in any common area or on the grounds, and will not use or permit the Premises to be used to facilitate criminal activity anywhere. Criminal activity includes, without limitation, a violation of Wyoming's controlled substances laws other than possession for personal use; an act of violence or a threat of violence against any person; unlawful possession or discharge of a weapon; and any other criminal conduct that threatens the health, safety or peaceful enjoyment of the property by another resident, by a neighbor, or by Landlord's employees or agents.\n\nNotification and cooperation. Tenant will tell Landlord in writing within a reasonable time of learning that any such conduct has occurred on the property, and will cooperate with Landlord and with law enforcement about it.\n\nBreach and remedy. Each obligation in this section is a lawful requirement of this Lease within the meaning of Wyo. Stat. § 1-21-1204(a)(vii). A violation by Tenant, by any occupant, or by a guest or other person under Tenant's control is a material breach of this Lease, for which Landlord may terminate this Lease and bring an action for forcible entry and detainer under Wyo. Stat. § 1-21-1002(a)(vi), after serving the three (3) day written notice to quit that Wyo. Stat. § 1-21-1003 requires. Proof is by the civil standard applied in that action; no arrest, charge or conviction is required, and an arrest, a charge or a criminal record is not by itself a violation of this section. Landlord's failure to act on one violation does not waive Landlord's rights as to any other.\n\nNothing in this section permits Landlord to take possession, change a lock or remove Tenant's property except through the process Wyoming law requires.",
